/* book Worked example 2: Q: A 1 Gbit/s NRZ link uses 20 km of standard single-mode fiber at 1550 nm (D = 17 ps/(nm·km)) and a Fabry–Perot laser of spectral width 2 nm. The transmitter rise time is 250 ps and the receiver rise time 350 ps. The launched power is 0 dBm, the sensitivity −28 dBm, α = 0.22 dB/km, with no joints or margin. Does the link pass? If not, find the longest fiber the rise-time budget allows and compare it with the attenuation limit.
   1. Allowed: Tb = 1000 ps, so tsys ≤ 700 ps. tchrom = 17 × 20 × 2 = 680 ps; tmod = 0.
   2. tsys = √(250² + 680² + 350²) = √647 400 = 804.6 ps > 700 ps: it fails, and could carry only 0.7 ÷ 804.6 ps = 0.87 Gbit/s NRZ.
   3. Allowed fiber rise time: √(700² − 250² − 350²) = √(490 000 − 62 500 − 122 500) = √305 000 = 552.3 ps.
   4. Longest length from rise time: L = 552.3 ÷ (17 × 2) = 16.2 km.
   5. Attenuation limit: Latt = 28 ÷ 0.22 = 127 km. At 20 km the fiber loses only 4.4 dB of the 28 dB available.
   Answer: It fails (804.6 ps against 700 ps). Rise time limits the reach to 16.2 km, far below the 127 km loss limit; a 0.1 nm DFB laser (34 ps) would pass. */
