/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Light leaves a fiber in a cone whose half-angle is the acceptance angle θa, so NA = sin θa.
   - A is the far-field spot diameter on the screen and D is the distance from the fiber end to the screen, in the same unit; tan θa = (A/2)/D.
   - The index profile gives NA = √(n1² − n2²) (core n1, cladding n2), which agrees only approximately: the far-field value depends on the launch, so it must fill the modes steadily, with cladding modes stripped and a few metres of fiber.
   - The cutoff wavelength λc is the wavelength above which only the fundamental mode LP01 is guided.
   - Cutoff depends on length and bending, so the method must be stated.
   - The cladding diameter (125 µm for telecom fiber) and core concentricity are measured on a calibrated video microscope image of the end face, or, during drawing, by timing the shadow of a scanning laser beam.
   - Every joint reflects some light back toward the laser.
*/
